  "base_commit": "4acf8386c481cd3ebe0b1857ccaeb2e6c11d3367",
  "pr_number": 2670,
  "issue_number": 2666,
  "issue_title": "[Feature]: Implement Epic keyword runtime (copy spell each upkeep and lock future spell casts)",
  "issue_body": "# [Feature]: Implement Epic keyword runtime (copy spell each upkeep and lock future spell casts)\n\n## Description\nThe **Epic** keyword is parsed and typed (`Keyword::Epic`), but it has no runtime behavior. The only current references are keyword parsing/display/coverage entries:\n\n- `types/keywords.rs` has `Keyword::Epic`.\n- `parser/oracle_keyword.rs` can display it as `\"epic\"`.\n- `game/coverage.rs` recognizes the keyword line.\n\nThere is no keyword synthesis, no resolution hook, no persistent \"you can't cast spells for the rest of the game\" state, and no beginning-of-upkeep copy trigger. As a result, an Epic spell resolves like an ordinary spell and then disappears; the defining rest-of-game effect never starts.\n\nThis is a good larger runtime feature because the engine already has pieces to reuse: spell-copy effects, target retargeting for copied spells, upkeep triggers, cast restrictions, and spell history/state plumbing. Epic needs those pieces connected through a persistent per-player runtime record.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n1. Cast an Epic spell, for example **Enduring Ideal**.\n2. Let it resolve.\n3. Advance to that player's next upkeep.\n4. No copy of the Epic spell is created.\n5. The player can still cast spells after the Epic spell resolved.\n\n## Expected behavior (CR 702.50)\nEpic has two linked runtime effects:\n\n- For the rest of the game, the spell's controller **can't cast spells**.\n- At the beginning of each of that player's upkeeps, that player copies the Epic spell except for its Epic ability, and may choose new targets for the copy.\n\nKey points:\n\n- The copy engine should not create a fresh Epic loop from the copy; the copy excludes Epic.\n- The copy is created at the beginning of each upkeep and is not cast, so ordinary \"when you cast\" triggers must not fire.\n- The cast lock applies to spells, not activated abilities or triggered abilities.\n- Multiple Epic spells can exist in unusual cases; each resolved Epic spell should create its own upkeep copy source, while the cast lock remains true for that player.\n\n## Actual behavior\n`Keyword::Epic` is inert:\n\n- No trigger is synthesized for the Epic keyword in `database/synthesis.rs`.\n- `stack.rs` resolution does not detect resolved Epic spells and does not register an ongoing Epic effect.\n- `GameState` has no persistent Epic record for the resolved spell snapshot / controller.\n- `game/restrictions.rs` does not block future spell casting after an Epic spell resolves.\n- Upkeep trigger collection has no Epic source to place a spell copy on the stack.\n\n## Impact\nAll printed Epic cards have their central mechanic missing:\n\n- **Enduring Ideal**\n- **Eternal Dominion**\n- **Neverending Torment**\n- **Undying Flames**\n- **Endless Swarm**\n\nThese cards are unavoidably wrong without Epic runtime. The first resolution happens, but the rest-of-game lock and recurring upkeep copies are the whole point of the mechanic.\n\n## Implementation\nImplement Epic as a persistent runtime record installed when an Epic spell resolves.\n\n- **`types/game_state.rs`**\n  - Add an `epic_effects` collection keyed by controller / source spell snapshot.\n  - Store enough data to make an upkeep copy later: controller, original spell name/card identity, copied `AbilityDefinition`, target data, modal choices if needed, and the turn/order metadata needed for deterministic trigger ordering.\n  - Add an `epic_locked_players` set or derive the lock from `epic_effects`.\n\n- **`game/stack.rs`**\n  - When a spell with effective `Keyword::Epic` resolves, register the Epic effect before the spell leaves the stack.\n  - Snapshot the resolved spell's ability/targets in a form the existing copy-spell resolver can reuse.\n  - Strip `Keyword::Epic` from the copied spell characteristics so upkeep copies do not recursively create new Epic records.\n\n- **`game/restrictions.rs` / cast availability**\n  - If a player is Epic-locked, reject any attempt to cast a spell.\n  - Keep activated abilities, triggered abilities, and resolving copied spells legal.\n  - Add a direct restriction test so the lock is enforced even if a UI path tries to cast manually.\n\n- **`game/triggers.rs` or a small `game/epic.rs` helper**\n  - At the beginning of each locked player's upkeep, create one trigger/action for each stored Epic effect that player controls.\n  - Resolve it by copying the stored spell onto the stack without casting it.\n  - Reuse existing target-copy / choose-new-targets machinery where possible.\n\n- **`database/synthesis.rs`**\n  - Add a `synthesize_epic` hook only if needed to give Epic spells a marker trigger/ability shape. If the cleaner path is stack-resolution detection, keep synthesis minimal and document that Epic is handled by `stack.rs`.\n\n- **Tests**\n  - Parser/type sanity: `Epic` remains parsed as `Keyword::Epic`.\n  - Resolution: resolving an Epic spell registers exactly one persistent Epic effect.\n  - Cast lock: the controller cannot cast spells after the Epic spell resolves, but can still activate abilities.\n  - Upkeep: the next upkeep creates a copy of the stored spell without casting it.\n  - Copy behavior: the upkeep copy does not contain Epic and does not register a second Epic effect when it resolves.\n  - Target behavior: a targeted Epic spell copy can reuse or retarget through the existing copy-spell target flow.\n\n## Component\n- `crates/engine/src/types/game_state.rs` - no persistent Epic state / lock.\n- `crates/engine/src/game/stack.rs` - resolving `Keyword::Epic` spells does not register the Epic effect.\n- `crates/engine/src/game/restrictions.rs` - no \"can't cast spells for the rest of the game\" gate.\n- `crates/engine/src/game/triggers.rs` or new `crates/engine/src/game/epic.rs` - no beginning-of-upkeep Epic copy generation.\n- `crates/engine/src/database/synthesis.rs` - no Epic keyword runtime hook.\n\n## Token-score fit\nThis should be a stronger token-score candidate than a small parser or one-card rules bug. A proper PR would add meaningful source tokens across game state, stack resolution, cast restriction enforcement, upkeep trigger/copy logic, and focused tests. The implementation is also dense runtime logic rather than padding: each source change is tied directly to a distinct CR 702.50 requirement.\n\nCR 702.50.\n",
